WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.583
YURI GRINSHTEYN:
During our last visit

00:00:01.583 --> 00:00:04.050
we used Stackdriver Trace
to diagnose a latency issue

00:00:04.050 --> 00:00:05.170
in our application.

00:00:05.170 --> 00:00:08.700
Today, we'll use Profiler
to dig into a service

00:00:08.700 --> 00:00:10.990
and see what could be
making it run slowly.

00:00:10.990 --> 00:00:12.450
This is the Stack Doctor.

00:00:12.450 --> 00:00:15.712
[THEME MUSIC]

00:00:18.023 --> 00:00:19.940
The last time you were
here, we found an issue

00:00:19.940 --> 00:00:22.070
where a bug was
causing high latency.

00:00:22.070 --> 00:00:23.870
Today, we'll dig into
a particular service

00:00:23.870 --> 00:00:24.890
with Profiler.

00:00:24.890 --> 00:00:28.100
Whereas Trace tells us how
long service calls take,

00:00:28.100 --> 00:00:29.720
Profiler is going
to show us what's

00:00:29.720 --> 00:00:31.430
happening within each service.

00:00:31.430 --> 00:00:33.020
Let's start digging in.

00:00:33.020 --> 00:00:35.450
We have a few services
that are being profiled.

00:00:35.450 --> 00:00:38.000
Let's pick the frontend
service since any issues there

00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:40.790
will directly impact
user experience.

00:00:40.790 --> 00:00:42.788
Let's use the CPU Time
metric to understand

00:00:42.788 --> 00:00:44.330
how our service
might be bottlenecked

00:00:44.330 --> 00:00:45.800
by CPU utilization.

00:00:45.800 --> 00:00:49.010
Profiler takes random sample
profiles of the system

00:00:49.010 --> 00:00:51.620
and combines the data to
show you what functions

00:00:51.620 --> 00:00:53.700
are using the most resources.

00:00:53.700 --> 00:00:55.580
The graph shows
the function calls

00:00:55.580 --> 00:00:59.690
grouped by their use of the
resource-- in this case, CPU,

00:00:59.690 --> 00:01:02.900
where the x-axis is
the amount of CPU

00:01:02.900 --> 00:01:06.230
and the y-axis shows
parent-child relationships.

00:01:06.230 --> 00:01:08.750
In this case, the
majority of the CPU

00:01:08.750 --> 00:01:11.480
is used by the ServeHTTP call.

00:01:11.480 --> 00:01:14.660
The expanded view shows
almost half of this

00:01:14.660 --> 00:01:18.680
is caused by viewCartHandler,
which in turn is mostly

00:01:18.680 --> 00:01:20.780
caused by getRecommendations.

00:01:20.780 --> 00:01:23.850
Now we know exactly
where the time is going.

00:01:23.850 --> 00:01:26.570
If this is the result of a
recently deployed change,

00:01:26.570 --> 00:01:28.700
we can roll it back
and let the engineering

00:01:28.700 --> 00:01:31.070
team know exactly
where the problem is

00:01:31.070 --> 00:01:33.590
so they can easily fix it.

00:01:33.590 --> 00:01:35.630
Sure glad we got
that taken care of.

00:01:35.630 --> 00:01:38.460
Now, you may be thinking,
sure, that looks easy.

00:01:38.460 --> 00:01:40.490
But how much work will
it be to get Profiler

00:01:40.490 --> 00:01:42.540
to work with my applications?

00:01:42.540 --> 00:01:44.640
Well, it turns out that
it's not much work at all.

00:01:44.640 --> 00:01:46.543
Let's take a look.

00:01:46.543 --> 00:01:48.710
No matter which language
your service is written in,

00:01:48.710 --> 00:01:51.410
it's easy to instrument
your code with Profiler.

00:01:51.410 --> 00:01:53.420
Simply install the
Profiler package

00:01:53.420 --> 00:01:55.520
for the language your
service is written in,

00:01:55.520 --> 00:01:58.730
import it in your code, and
then call the start method.

00:01:58.730 --> 00:02:01.490
Even better, you can specify
the version of your service

00:02:01.490 --> 00:02:03.410
you're profiling
so that later, you

00:02:03.410 --> 00:02:06.320
can compare profiles created
from different versions

00:02:06.320 --> 00:02:09.500
and understand how code pushes
are impacting infrastructure

00:02:09.500 --> 00:02:12.420
utilization and performance.

00:02:12.420 --> 00:02:14.420
Now, one thing you
should be aware of

00:02:14.420 --> 00:02:18.380
is that adding Profiler to
Java code is a bit different.

00:02:18.380 --> 00:02:20.960
You need to pass the path to
the library as an argument

00:02:20.960 --> 00:02:22.310
when you start your JVM.

00:02:22.310 --> 00:02:24.800
Once your code is
instrumented with Profiler,

00:02:24.800 --> 00:02:27.470
profiles will be shipped
to the Stackdriver backend.

00:02:27.470 --> 00:02:29.360
From there, you
can start analyzing

00:02:29.360 --> 00:02:32.300
how your code is using
system resources right away

00:02:32.300 --> 00:02:35.690
and fixing problems like
the one we dealt with today.

00:02:35.690 --> 00:02:37.670
Now that you've learned
how to use Profiler,

00:02:37.670 --> 00:02:39.980
you'll be much better
prepared to optimize

00:02:39.980 --> 00:02:43.770
your service for resource
utilization and performance.

00:02:43.770 --> 00:02:45.050
That's it for this episode.

00:02:45.050 --> 00:02:47.480
Come back soon for more on
how Stackdriver can help you

00:02:47.480 --> 00:02:49.460
keep your application healthy.

00:02:49.460 --> 00:02:50.840
This is the Stack Doctor.

00:02:50.840 --> 00:02:51.860
Stay healthy out there.

00:02:51.860 --> 00:02:55.210
[THEME MUSIC]

